<h1 style="color: #1a5276; text-align: center;">Lesson Planner – Theme # This Is a Local Artist. – Lesson # 1</h1>
<p style="text-align: center; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Complete this planner five times per theme, once per lesson (Lesson 1 through Lesson 5).</p>

<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Lesson #:</b> 1</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Skills Focus:</b> Listening</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Grade:</b> 7th Grade (CEFR: A2.2)</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Scenario:</b> Arts and Crafts</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Theme:</b> This Is a Local Artist.</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"><b>Date(s):</b> From ___ to ___</td>
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="2"><b>Learning Sequence Time:</b> 45–50 min</td>
    </tr>
    <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
        <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;" colspan="3">
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Specific Objective:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend multi-step instructions for academic tasks. Interactive: Respond to questions and requests in a school setting.</p>
            <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Learning Outcome:</b> Listening: Can show comprehension of descriptions inspired by local artists or related to their work to complete an arts and crafts project (e.g., "Prepare materials for a painting inspired by Olga Sinclair's colorful murals"). Can respond to questions and requests related to arts and crafts in the school setting (e.g., "Who is your favorite artist?" What are some of their works?). Can summarize a peer's description of an artwork (e.g., "It is a painting that depicts Panamanian rural life").</p>
        </td>
    </tr>
</table>

<h2 style="color: #1a5276;">The Six Action-Oriented Approach (AOA) Lesson Stages</h2>
<table style="border: 1px dashed #333; border-collapse: collapse; width: 100%; margin: 10px 0;">
    <thead>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Stage & Description</th>
            <th style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 34%;">Activities & Teacher Actions</th>
            <th style="1px dashed #333; padding: 8px; width: 33%;">Estimated Date and Time</th>
        </tr>
    </thead>
    <tbody>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 1 — Warm-up / Pre-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Engagement, Modeling and Clarification.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Warm-up:</b> The teacher plays a short audio clip featuring sounds of various art forms being created (e.g., painting strokes, chiseling for sculpture, fabric weaving). Students listen and guess what type of art they hear. The teacher then shows images of different Panamanian artworks (e.g., a mola, a tagua carving, a mural) and asks, "What kind of art is this?" to activate prior knowledge related to "Arts and Crafts."</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Modeling:</b> The teacher introduces 3-4 core vocabulary words (e.g., <i>artist, mural, sculpture, creative, vibrant</i>) by pointing to the images. The teacher says each word clearly, modeling pronunciation and syllable stress, for example: "This is a <i>mural</i>. It is very <i>vibrant</i>." "This <i>artist</i> has <i>created</i> a beautiful <i>sculpture</i>." (e.g., /skʌlp.tʃər/, stress on the first syllable). The teacher models present perfect and present simple to describe achievements and art. Students repeat chorally and individually.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Clarification (CCQs):</b> The teacher checks understanding of the new vocabulary.
                    <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"Is a <i>mural</i> a small painting or a large painting on a wall?" (Expected answer: A large painting on a wall.)</li>
                        <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">"If something is <i>vibrant</i>, is it dull or full of bright colors?" (Expected answer: Full of bright colors.)</li>
                    </ul>
                </p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 2 — Presentation</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Input of new language/content connected to Scenario: "Arts and Crafts."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Material:</b> The teacher plays an audio recording of a short segment from an interview with a local Panamanian artisan describing their craft (e.g., how to make a basic *mola* or *tagua carving*) and the materials involved. The audio also briefly describes an *exhibition* in a local *gallery* where their work is *displayed*. (Genre: interview segment/informal instructions; Register: informative; Format: audio recording).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Competence:</b> Listening: Receptive: Comprehend multi-step instructions for academic tasks. Interactive: Respond to questions and requests in a school setting.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Comprehension tasks:</b> Students listen to the audio recording for the first time.</p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Gist Questions:</b> "What kind of craft does the artist talk about?" "Where does the artist display their work?"</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>True/False Statements:</b> Students listen again and decide if statements about the artist's work or process are True or False. Example: "The artist makes *sculptures*." (False). "The artist has *displayed* their work in a *gallery*." (True).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;"><b>Information Matching:</b> Students listen a third time and match new vocabulary words (e.g., <i>mola, tagua carving, gallery, exhibition, display, create, design</i>) to definitions or images provided by the teacher on a handout.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 3 — Preparation / Practice</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Accuracy focus on Listening.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Communicative Skill & Content:</b> Listening comprehension of descriptions and multi-step instructions, recognition of target vocabulary (artist, craft, basket, sculpture, gallery, studio, exhibition, canvas, building, mural, mola, tagua carving; create, paint, sculpt, design, carve, display; creative, artistic, handmade, colorful, abstract, bold, vibrant, minimalist, timeless, unique, detailed; skillfully, originally) and grammatical structures (Present Perfect, Present and Past Simple, Imperatives). Pronunciation focus on syllable stress (<i>sculpture, design</i>) and inflectional endings (<i>-ed, -ing</i>).</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Detailed Procedure:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1.  <b>Controlled Practice (Listening Gap-fill):</b> Students receive a cloze activity with sentences extracted from the audio (or similar descriptions/instructions). They listen to the audio again and fill in the blanks with the correct target vocabulary or grammar form. Example: "This *artist* has ______ (create) many *colorful* *molas*." "First, you ______ (prepare) the materials and then you ______ (soak) the fibers." The teacher specifically highlights '-ed' and '-ing' endings in pronunciation.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2.  <b>Semi-controlled (Listen and Sequence/Identify):</b> The teacher provides a list of jumbled steps or materials (with images) related to the craft described in the audio. Students listen to the instructions again and reorder the steps or identify the correct materials mentioned, practicing comprehension of imperatives. Example: "Listen for the steps: <i>First, you prepare the fibers. Second, you begin to weave. Third, you add the colors.</i> Now, put these pictures in order."</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">3.  <b>Pair Interaction/Peer Check (Listen and Describe/Respond):</b> In pairs, Student A describes a simple piece of art (using target vocabulary like <i>colorful, handmade, created</i> and past/present simple) inspired by a local artist, and Student B listens and draws what they hear. Then, Student B asks 1-2 questions about the description or materials (e.g., "What colors did you mention? Has this *artist* displayed their work in a *studio*?"). Student A responds, showing interactive listening.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 4 — Performance / Production</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Communicative task where students USE the language in a realistic scenario: "Arts and Crafts."</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Task: "Art Project Materials & Steps Organizer."</b> Students work in pairs. The teacher provides an audio recording (or reads a script) of multi-step instructions for preparing materials for a specific local Panamanian craft project (e.g., "Making a Small Mola Design" or "Carving a Simple Tagua Piece"). The instructions incorporate imperatives and target vocabulary. Student A listens to the instructions and fills out an "Art Project Materials & Steps Organizer" worksheet, listing the required materials and the first 3-4 steps. Student B, acting as a "project manager," listens simultaneously and then asks Student A follow-up questions to clarify or confirm the instructions (e.g., "What materials do we need for the *mola*? What is the first step to *carve* the *tagua*?"). Student A must respond based on their comprehension, using target vocabulary and grammatical structures, and demonstrating their receptive and interactive listening skills.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Format:</b> Paired activity involving sustained listening, information transfer (filling out a worksheet), and interactive Q&A.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to Theme/Scenario:</b> Directly engages students with the "Arts and Crafts" scenario by focusing on preparing for a project, and the "Local Artist" theme by using Panamanian craft examples.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Focus:</b> Fluency in listening for details and multi-step instructions, accurate comprehension, and interactive communication to clarify information.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 5 — Assessment / Post-task</h3>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;">Formative assessment strategy.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Formative assessment strategy:</b> Teacher observation checklist during the "Art Project Materials & Steps Organizer" task and subsequent Q&A. The teacher observes how accurately students fill out the organizer and how effectively they respond to and ask questions.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Assessment Task:</b> Students submit their completed "Art Project Materials & Steps Organizer" worksheet. The teacher also monitors student interactions during the Q&A phase of the production task.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Evaluation Criteria (Listening-focused):</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can correctly identify and list at least 3 materials mentioned in the audio instructions.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Can correctly sequence at least 2 of the initial steps from the audio instructions (imperatives).</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Responds intelligibly and appropriately to peer's comprehension questions, using relevant vocabulary.</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">Demonstrates understanding of descriptions related to art/artists using at least 3 target vocabulary words (e.g., <i>mola, creative, prepare</i>).</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student Self-Assessment / Exit Ticket:</b> Students complete an exit ticket with the following prompts:</p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. What was the most challenging instruction or description to understand today?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. Name one new art-related word you heard today that you can now use to describe an *artist*'s work.</li>
                </ul>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
        <tr style="border: 1px dashed #333;">
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <h3 style="color: #1a5276; margin-top: 0;">STAGE 6 — Reflection</h3>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;">
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Student self-reflection prompt:</b></p>
                <ul style="margin-left: 20px; padding: 0;">
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">1. How well did I understand the multi-step instructions for the art project? What strategies did I use to help my listening comprehension?</li>
                    <li style="margin-bottom: 4px; text-align: justify;">2. What new vocabulary words related to "Arts and Crafts" did I learn, and how confident am I in recognizing them when I hear them?</li>
                </ul>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Teacher reflection:</b> Did the students effectively comprehend the multi-step instructions and descriptions of local artists' work? Were the audio materials at an appropriate CEFR A2.2 level? Did the vocabulary and grammar activities (Present Perfect, Imperatives) support listening accuracy? What specific challenges did students face (e.g., speed of delivery, unfamiliar vocabulary, complex sentences)? For the next lesson, I will ensure more visual aids accompany new vocabulary and consider pre-teaching specific phrases from the reading text to support comprehension.</p>
                <p style="text-align: justify; margin: 4px 0 8px 0;"><b>Connection to next lesson in the theme sequence:</b> Today, we focused on listening to understand descriptions and instructions about local art. Tomorrow, we will transition to "Reading Comprehension" (Lesson 2 of 5) by reading short biographical texts about Panamanian artists and descriptions of their *creations* to deepen our understanding of the theme and reinforce the newly introduced *vocabulary*.</p>
            </td>
            <td style="border: 1px dashed #333; padding: 8px;"></td>
        </tr>
    </tbody>
</table>